! Silicon with one atom pushed off its site, so that there is a force to compare.
!
! The reference case for P15. Everything about it is the two-atom cell of
! `pw_scf/scf.in` except that the second atom is moved by 0.02 alat along x,
! which lowers the symmetry from 48 operations to 8 and gives every atom a force
! along one axis and nothing along the other two -- so the test is not only
! "does the number match" but "does the symmetry come out right".
!
! The k-points are an automatic grid rather than the two explicit ones of
! scf.in: an explicit list chosen for the undistorted crystal is not an
! irreducible set for the distorted one, and comparing two codes on a k-set that
! is wrong for the structure tests the wrongness rather than the force.
!
! conv_thr is tightened far past what the answer needs because QE's printed force
! includes its `force_corr` term, which corrects for a density that has stopped
! short of self-consistency and which has no counterpart in a force obtained by
! differentiating the energy at the fixed point. At 1e-10 it is ~1e-8 Ry/bohr and
! the comparison is between the same two quantities.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
    tprnfor = .true.
    verbosity = 'high'
 /
 &system
    ibrav=2, celldm(1)=10.20, nat=2, ntyp=1,
    ecutwfc=12.0
 /
 &electrons
    conv_thr = 1.0d-10
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 Si  28.086  Si.pz-vbc.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS (alat)
 Si 0.00 0.00 0.00
 Si 0.27 0.25 0.25
K_POINTS (automatic)
 4 4 4 0 0 0
